rows='''| F124 | **Argon2 (RFC 9106, Informational, September 2021):** Argon2id is the variant to choose when unsure; first recommended option t=1, p=4, m=2^21 KiB (2 GiB), 128-bit salt, 256-bit tag; second recommended option for less memory t=3, p=4, m=2^16 KiB (64 MiB). Salt 128 bits suffices (sec 4, 7.4). Together with OWASP's floor of m=19456 KiB, t=2, p=1 (bt-F421) this gives a sourced range; **the value for the production VPS is not decided by any source** and needs the tuning run S13. | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9106.html (fetched 2026-10-19; fetch-tool summary) | 1 | The RFC's second option is far above the OWASP floor; a 4 GB VPS shared with PostgreSQL limits concurrent verifies (inference). S13 is **PENDING - S13** (no VPS measurement; the VPS size is not in the repository: NOT SOURCEABLE). |
| F125 | **OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet:** idle timeout about 2-5 minutes for high-value and 15-30 minutes for low-risk applications; absolute timeout example 4-8 hours for an all-day office application; timeouts enforced on the server; renew the session ID after any privilege change (password, permission, admin switch); re-authenticate for high-risk events (password or email change, new device, recovery); `__Host-` prefix, Secure, HttpOnly, explicit SameSite (Strict preferred); at least 64 bits of entropy (128 bits for custom IDs); on a new login either end the older session or ask; give users a session list and remote termination; no persistent cookies unless needed. | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html (fetched 2026-10-19; fetch-tool summary) | 4 | Guidance. The tier-1 numbers are NIST's (F107). The two tiers differ by a large factor (NIST AAL1 30 days vs OWASP 15-30 min idle for low-risk): stated conflict, resolved by the owner's risk decision in the ADR (ASVS 7.1.1 asks only for documented justification). |
| F126 | **OWASP Multifactor Authentication Cheat Sheet:** require MFA for all users and in particular admin accounts; offering TOTP as opt-in is listed as an option; step-up for changing credentials or contact details, disabling MFA and elevating to admin; change factors only after re-authenticating with an already enrolled factor, not on the session alone, and notify out of band, "consider delays" for high-value accounts; recovery and fallback methods must not be weaker than the primary MFA; with WebAuthn verify the user-verification flag on the server (presence alone is not a factor) and do not downgrade to weaker methods; lost-all-factors options are recovery codes, several enrolled factors, a mailed one-use code, identity proofing by support, vouching, with no single best method; SMS is a "restricted" authenticator (SIM swap, porting, SS7), needs per-account rate limits against cost abuse and a migration plan; email as a factor is debated. | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Multifactor_Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html (fetched 2026-10-19; fetch-tool summary) | 4 | Guidance; supports ASVS 6.4.3/6.4.4/6.6.1/7.5.1 (F102, F103). The page does not address synced passkeys (refers to a separate Passkey Security cheat sheet, not fetched: NOT SOURCEABLE, budget). |
| F127 | **Repository standard: ASVS verification level is Level 2 for all products and Level 3 for the auth, billing and licensing modules.** So for this module the L3 rows apply: 6.3.3 at L3 (a hardware-based, phishing-resistant factor), 6.3.5-6.3.8, 6.5.6, 6.5.8, 6.6.1 ("phone and SMS must not be available as options" at L3), 7.5.3. ADR-0004 section 7a / header records that the founder keeps the superuser credential in his Bitwarden and that the operator-id weak spot is recorded, not fixed. | `.claude/docs/security-baseline.md` line 10; `docs/architecture/adr-0004-operator-access.md` line 29 (read 2026-10-19) | repo (not a source tier) | Decisive for area B: with L3 and 6.6.1, SMS OTP for the auth module is out unless the founder records a deviation (ADR-0005's archive-contact path and rm- section 7 show deviations are recorded by name). Whether L3 applies to the dashboard sign-in or only to the operator console is a question for security-lead (Open question). |
'''
